C = [
("h1", "6. One Trespass, One Righteousness"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 5:18–19 finishes the comparison that verse 12 began, and it is the place where the words “all men” and “many” carry the most weight. The volume reads them clause by clause. It does not claim that Paul says every person is justified, and it does not claim that he says otherwise. It reads the sentence with the clause of reception that stands just before it, in the same parenthesis."),
("sub", "6.1 Verse 18: the comparison resumed"),
("p", "“Therefore as by the offence of one judgment came upon all men to condemnation; even so by the righteousness of one the free gift came upon all men unto justification of life” (Romans 5:18). The sentence is short and balanced. It has two halves joined by “as” and “even so,” and the half that was missing in verse 12 is here supplied. Judgment came upon all men to condemnation, by one offence. The free gift came upon all men unto justification of life, by one righteousness."),
("p", "The two halves use the same words: “by,” “one,” “came upon all men,” and an end-word that begins with “unto” or “to.” The end-words are “condemnation” and “justification of life.” The sentence is plainly constructed so that the second half answers the first. What is being said about the first half, the reader is meant to hear of the second."),
("sub", "6.2 What “all men” does in the first half"),
("p", "In the first half, “all men” is the same group that appears in verse 12: “death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned.” It is the whole human race, in the sense that no one is outside it. Paul has spoken of all men in this sense since the first chapter of the letter, where the wrath is against “all ungodliness and unrighteousness of men” (Romans 1:18), and where “all the world may become guilty before God” (Romans 3:19). The phrase in verse 18, first half, is of a piece with these."),
("sub", "6.3 What “all men” does in the second half"),
("p", "In the second half, the same words are used. Whether they have the same extent in both halves is the question on which the readings divide, and the volume sets out the three ways in which they are commonly taken so that the reader can see what is at stake."),
("ctbl", "Three ways of reading “all men” in the second half of 5:18.", [
    ("Reading", "What it says", "What the volume observes"),
    ("Every person is in fact justified", "The free gift came upon every human being, with the result that all are justified", "Verse 17 describes those who reign in life as “they which receive,” and the sentence of 5:18 is not set against it. The passage does not say that every person receives"),
    ("The gift is available to all", "The free gift came upon all men in the sense that it is offered to every human being", "This is a possible sense of “came upon,” but the sentence does not use the words //offered// or //available//. It says what came"),
    ("All who are in the second man", "“All men” in the second half means all who belong to the one by whom the gift came, just as “all men” in the first half means all who belong to the one by whom the judgment came", "This reading keeps the symmetry of the sentence and gives “they which receive” its place. Its difficulty is that the words “all men” are the same in both halves"),
  ], [0.22, 0.38, 0.40]),
("p", "The volume does not claim that the sentence settles which of the three is meant, and it does not rest its thesis on any one of them. It makes a narrower claim. The sentence is part of a parenthesis in which verse 17 has already told the reader that those who reign in life are “they which receive.” A reading of verse 18 that ignores verse 17 has dropped a clause of Paul’s own argument. A reading that keeps it must say that the “all men” of the second half are connected with receiving."),
("p", "There is a parallel in the same letter. In chapter 3 Paul says that the righteousness of God is “by faith of Jesus Christ unto all and upon all them that believe” (Romans 3:22). Here the word “all” is joined, in the same sentence, to “them that believe.” Paul can say “all” and mean all within a stated limit. He does so in Romans 3:22, and he states the limit. In Romans 5:17 he states a limit as well. Whether the “all men” of 5:18 sits inside the limit of 5:17 is the point at issue, and the volume holds that the sentence is easier to read when it does."),
("sub", "6.4 Verse 19: the many"),
("p", "“For as by one man’s disobedience many were made sinners, so by the obedience of one shall many be made righteous” (Romans 5:19). Verse 19 gives the reason for verse 18 (“For”), and it changes the word from “all men” to “many.” In the first half, the many “were made sinners” by one man’s disobedience. In the second half, “by the obedience of one shall many be made righteous.” The verbs differ in tense: “were made” is past, and “shall…be made” is future."),
("p", "Two observations. First, “all men” of verse 18 and “many” of verse 19 are used for the same group in the first half. Death passed upon all men (verse 12); many be dead (verse 15); judgment came upon all men (verse 18); many were made sinners (verse 19). Paul moves between the words “all” and “many” without marking a change of group, and the reader is entitled to think that he does the same in the second half. Second, the future tense of “shall many be made righteous” looks ahead of the moment of writing. What is said of the first man’s result is said as a thing that has happened; what is said of the second is said as a thing that shall be."),
("tbl", "“All” and “many” in Romans 5.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Rom 5:12", "“death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned”", "“All” twice: death passes to all; all have sinned"),
    ("Rom 5:15", "“through the offence of one many be dead”", "“Many” for the group that died"),
    ("Rom 5:17", "“they which receive abundance of grace”", "The second headship is described with a clause of reception"),
    ("Rom 5:18", "“judgment came upon all men to condemnation”", "“All men” in the first half"),
    ("Rom 5:18", "“the free gift came upon all men unto justification of life”", "“All men” in the second half"),
    ("Rom 5:19", "“many were made sinners”", "“Many” for the first half"),
    ("Rom 5:19", "“shall many be made righteous”", "“Many” for the second half, future tense"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "6.5 What the volume takes from these two verses"),
("p", "The volume takes the following. The comparison begun in verse 12 is completed in verse 18: one offence, one righteousness; judgment to condemnation, the free gift to justification of life. The words “all” and “many” are interchangeable in the first half, and the second half uses them with the same freedom. The second headship is described in verse 17 as belonging to those who receive. The volume does not teach that every person is justified, because the passage that carries the words “all men” also carries the clause of reception. It does not teach that some are shut out of the gift in advance, because the passage does not say so. It leaves the reader with the words as Paul set them, and it asks that they be read together."),
]
